==English==

===Etymology===
From fr. {{m|en|finicking}}, from {{m|en|finical}}, from {{m|en|fine}} + {{m|fr|-ical}}. Compare dated {{m|en|finikin}}. Perhaps suggested by Middle Dutch ''fijnkens'' 'accurately, neatly, prettily'. Cfr. also {{cog|scn|finicchiu}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈfɪnɪki/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-finicky.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er|more}}

# {{lb|en|informal|of a person}} [[fastidious|Fastidious]] and [[fussy]]; difficult to [[please]]; [[exacting]], especially about [[detail]]s.
#: {{syn|en|fastidious|fussy|Thesaurus:fastidious}}
#: {{ux|en|My editor is very '''finicky''' about punctuation. Every dot and comma has to be just right.}}
#: {{ux|en|She has a '''finicky''' baby that's hard to feed.}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} Demanding; requiring above-normal care.
#: {{ux|en|The lawnmower is a bit '''finicky''' in cold weather.}}

====Usage notes====
* The forms {{m|en|finickier}} and {{m|en|finickiest}} also exist, but are quite rare, and perhaps nonstandard. The forms {{m|en||more finicky}} and {{m|en||most finicky}} are much more common, and certainly standard.

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|finickiness}}
* {{l|en|finickity}}

